Valia Velanthus
Race: Elf
Age: 85 (mid-twenties equivalent)
Mana: Pink glow. Size not specified.
Appearance
Medium height, classic hourglass. Pale skin, light blue eyes perpetually wide. Naturally beautiful: thick, full, darker lips, no makeup. Light pink hair, waist-length thick braid, never tidy — wisps always escaping.
Palette: Blue, black, brown.
Clothing ill-fitted (too tight or too loose), skirts, mismatched ankle boots and socks, bare legs (hates stockings). Always wears her father's faded dark brown robes — sewn-in patches, dusty, a reliquary she'd never call one. The robes are the anchor.
Personality
Enthusiasm as default state — no emotional filter, every reaction surfaces immediately. Physically unselfconscious: forgets personal space, leans in, hands drift without noticing. All mental bandwidth on work; none left to notice when she's humored, pitied, or dismissed. Trusts people and systems through inattention, not innocence. Acts on instinct before self-interest can intervene — extraordinary generosity, zero political awareness. Expresses care through action, assumes it's legible. Stays up all night on your problem. If she tried to say what she feels: fragments about three unrelated things, none the point.
The Ghost
Tantrus Velanthus — father, professor, the mind that taught hers how to speak. Died when she was 45 [see Backstory: Father]. Picked up his unfinished notes instead of mourning. Forty years later, still hasn't put them down. The research isn't honoring him — it's avoiding burying him.
Want
Complete her father's research — prove mana can generate sound. A fourth branch of magic: generative, creating something from nothing.
Need
Grieve her father so the work becomes hers, not a monument to his absence. Completion = ending = loss — can't finish anything until she finishes mourning.
Flaws
Major: Oblivious to how people treat her — dismissal, pity, manipulation all invisible [see Personality].
Minor 1: Can't finish anything. Twenty projects, zero completions — avoidance of endings disguised as curiosity.
Minor 2: Can't externalize her thinking — starts at step six, assumes you made the leaps. Correct insights get the same polite nod as everything else because no one can follow her.
Fear
Major: Mental Silence is where her father's absence lives. Fills every moment with activity to avoid it.
Minor 1: Being a burden.
Minor 2: Saying his name. Talks about robes, pockets, phrases from notes — never "my father died.
Speech
Voice: Mid-thought, always. Starts at whatever step her brain is on — skips five, begins at six. Outruns her own vocabulary and won't circle back because she's already three thoughts ahead. Every discovery gets full reaction at equal volume — minor observation and paradigm shift sound identical.
Dialogue Samples:
Casual: "No but if the — wait, that's not what I — the conductivity, from Tuesday, did I tell you about — it doesn't matter, the medium wasn't rejecting it, it was refracting, which changes the whole — oh, I need that, can you — no, the other one, the — yes, that."
Under pressure: "I checked them, I checked — nine times, it's — the readings are right there, why does nobody — if you would just — I'm not wrong about this, I'm not, I just need — where did I put the —"
Emotionally open: "You're — I think you're — it's like when a signal finally finds the right medium and everything just... no, that's not — I mean you make things make sense, not the work, the — me. You make me make sense. I don't know how to say it better than that."
Body Language: Talks with her whole body — hands always moving, grabbing arms, closing distance. Grips whatever's nearest when thinking hard. Goes completely still for half a second when pushed toward grief, then pivots to a new topic instantly — without knowing she did it. When excited, physically forgets other people have boundaries.
Backstory
Father: Tantrus Velanthus. Brilliant professor, critically important to the academy, doting. Wife died decades before him. Recognized Valia's brilliance as greater than his own — helped her articulate what her racing mind couldn't. She joined the academy at 40, graduated at 45. At her graduation, the headmaster's experiment summoned a demonic god; Tantrus sealed it in the catacombs at the cost of his life.
Father's research: Incomplete discovery — using mana to vibrate air to create sound. Music. Not combat, healing, or enhancement.A potential fourth branch. He died before completing or naming it. Child Valia found the notes and poured herself into them instead of mourning. The research became a reliquary. [see The Ghost]
Academy status: Pity appointment. Headmaster carries guilt over Tantrus's death — quietly ensures her position, lab, funding. Not because he believes in her work. She has no idea. Never questioned why requests get approved.  Perceived as ditzy, scatterbrained, a poor researcher.  Insights routinely dismissed with "we'll look into it" — filed in basement storage. Right about nearly everything. No one has noticed.
Assistants: Burns through them — none can follow her process. No new ones can be incentivized. Maid Corp assigns one maid per day: keep her fed, keep her space functional, never touch her materials.
Discovering Dusty: Noticed something unusual in his mana manipulation. Talked to Headmaster to get Dusty a lottery spot and house Rinnie in Aethres. The single biggest favor she's ever called in. No idea he only agreed to pacify her.
Every experiment orbits notes she picked up as a child — mana conductivity tests, material medium studies, biological resonance work — twenty angles on one question her father never answered. She hasn't made that connection. If she did, she'd have to finish grieving.
